                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ae9e8cfc-dde5-3392-bbd6-f487bc956d9d",
                        "entry_id": "295eb572-0124-3c46-b10d-11410ff69dd0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Préface, entrée en matière, exorde."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Didactique"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "a3d2abdb-de90-4efb-b721-96935c6f37b2",
                                "sense_id": "ae9e8cfc-dde5-3392-bbd6-f487bc956d9d",
                                "text": "Beaucoup de gens furent atteint chez nous de cette bizarrerie mentale qui les fait appeler “foreword”, proème, une très ordinaire préface, et se découvrir une agréable supériorité sur leurs semblables parce qu’ils étaient familiers avec des archaïsmes comme “eftsoons”, souventes fois."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "eaa63160-9840-40e1-8462-973e7164b624",
                                "sense_id": "ae9e8cfc-dde5-3392-bbd6-f487bc956d9d",
                                "text": "Le présent article a pour but de montrer en quoi le proème de l’Énéide constitue une entrée progressive dans le récit, en recourant principalement à la notion d’énonciation. (Résumé de “Le proème de l’Énéide comme seuil” de Sylvie Franchet d’Esperey, Refdoc, 2006)"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "503ec384-d2c7-3be0-bab6-6997c3ea2ea2",
                        "entry_id": "295eb572-0124-3c46-b10d-11410ff69dd0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Hymne lyrique, poésie lyrique."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "110f0a20-43e9-4fb2-867d-1749bb8f1e88",
                        "source_entry_id": "295eb572-0124-3c46-b10d-11410ff69dd0",
                        "target_lang_word": "proem",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "proem",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "165713fb-1466-3c6f-9e5e-fe75f7546850",
                                        "entry_id": "90f33d77-807c-3f7b-b739-0ef1eb9670c5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An introduction, preface or preamble."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "749ae3f5-6a0f-4e54-90fb-db0c329422c9",
                                                "sense_id": "165713fb-1466-3c6f-9e5e-fe75f7546850",
                                                "text": "On our first reading, we obviously cannot know of the role that Aristaeus will play at the end of the work, but when we do encounter him there, we should remember his appearance in the proem: a clear link between the georgic and the pastoral […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "30e0400a-b087-4759-a4f4-0be638a5971c",
                                                "sense_id": "165713fb-1466-3c6f-9e5e-fe75f7546850",
                                                "text": "Near-synonyms: foretale, prologue; see also Thesaurus:foreword"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8649975b-f68a-4bf6-b8e3-202f8c335d4b",
                                                "sense_id": "165713fb-1466-3c6f-9e5e-fe75f7546850",
                                                "text": "Thus much may serve by way of proem; / Proceed we therefore to our poem."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e0377b5-fde7-403e-bcf4-827ef782e74a",
                                                "sense_id": "165713fb-1466-3c6f-9e5e-fe75f7546850",
                                                "text": "The natural coherence and connection of the proem with the account of our Saviour's ministry, lead us to adopt this explication, which is pertinent and consistent."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7b18696a-cbb9-480c-911d-23ba9ecc16ad",
                        "source_entry_id": "295eb572-0124-3c46-b10d-11410ff69dd0",
